00:41:16.106 --> 00:41:17.775
but ah, I'm sure you
hear it all the time."

00:41:17.775 --> 00:41:19.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I go, "well, I
hear it all the time,

00:41:19.643 --> 00:41:21.078
but on the other hand,

00:41:21.078 --> 00:41:24.682 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'd like you to just Google
Dennis Prager asshole

00:41:24.682 --> 00:41:26.417
and see how many hits you get."

00:41:27.918 --> 00:41:29.553 align:start size:84% position:16%
- There's worse things
you could google

00:41:29.553 --> 00:41:31.489
about either one
of us, I'm sure.

00:41:31.489 --> 00:41:32.823
You know that actually-

00:41:32.823 --> 00:41:33.958
- That is correct.

00:41:33.958 --> 00:41:35.426 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That actually
reminds me of something

00:41:35.426 --> 00:41:36.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I hope you don't
mind me bringing this up.

00:41:36.894 --> 00:41:39.396 align:start size:97% position:3%
When Jordan and I and you
had dinner a couple weeks ago

00:41:39.396 --> 00:41:41.232
and I was fortunate enough,

00:41:41.232 --> 00:41:42.600
I don't know how many
people would trade

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:43.868
almost everything they got

00:41:43.868 --> 00:41:45.369
to right before they're
about to be a father,

00:41:45.369 --> 00:41:47.505 align:start size:97% position:3%
to sit down with Dennis
Prager and Jordan Peterson.

00:41:47.505 --> 00:41:49.473
And one interesting
thing that we noticed

00:41:49.473 --> 00:41:51.542
between the three of us is that,

00:41:51.542 --> 00:41:54.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you don't mind me saying,
you are 74 years old.

00:41:54.845 --> 00:41:56.814
Jordan is 60 and I'm 46.

00:41:56.814 --> 00:41:59.783 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we have 14 years
between the three of us,

00:41:59.783 --> 00:42:01.185
which was just sort of-

00:42:01.185 --> 00:42:02.620
- Oh, that's interesting.

00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:04.088 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It was just sort of
interesting to think about.

00:42:04.088 --> 00:42:05.556 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you said something
when we were talking about

00:42:05.556 --> 00:42:07.791
how to parent and teach the
children the right thing

00:42:07.791 --> 00:42:08.993
and all this.

00:42:08.993 --> 00:42:10.928
You said something
to the effect of how,

00:42:10.928 --> 00:42:13.831
that whatever your
parents' faults were,

00:42:13.831 --> 00:42:16.166
whatever you might look
back and fault them for,

00:42:16.166 --> 00:42:18.903
that you said, but I turned
out to be a good moral person

00:42:18.903 --> 00:42:21.005
so I can let all
of those things go.

00:42:21.005 --> 00:42:22.373
I'm roughly paraphrasing.

00:42:22.373 --> 00:42:23.641
- That's right.

00:42:23.641 --> 00:42:25.543
- But I thought was
a really decent way

00:42:25.543 --> 00:42:28.746
of moving forward in life

00:42:28.746 --> 00:42:30.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
because everyone's got
issues with their parents,

00:42:30.681 --> 00:42:33.350
but a lot of people are just
focused on them all day long.

00:42:33.350 --> 00:42:38.055 align:start position:0%
- Which is why by the way,
another brilliance of the Torah.

00:42:38.055 --> 00:42:43.027
We're instructed to honor our
parents but not to love them.

00:42:44.228 --> 00:42:46.764
And we are told to
love the stranger,

00:42:46.764 --> 00:42:49.533 align:start size:81% position:19%
to love God, to
love our neighbor,

00:42:49.533 --> 00:42:51.835
but we're not told
to love our parents.

00:42:51.835 --> 00:42:56.807
It realizes in its genius that
cannot be demanded of people.

00:42:57.708 --> 00:42:59.076
It can be demanded of people

00:42:59.076 --> 00:43:01.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they not dishonor
their parents.

00:43:01.378 --> 00:43:03.314
This is why I tell you.

00:43:03.314 --> 00:43:06.517 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is reason number 8566 why
I'm in love with the Torah.

00:43:06.517 --> 00:43:08.852
- How do you square that
with people who just have,

00:43:08.852 --> 00:43:10.654 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, just generally
terrible parents.

00:43:10.654 --> 00:43:14.158 align:start position:0%
I mean alcoholic, abusive,
neglectful et cetera, et cetera.

00:43:14.158 --> 00:43:15.659
- That's right.

00:43:15.659 --> 00:43:20.431
So I spoke about that on
my Rosh Hashanah service.

00:43:22.366 --> 00:43:25.169
As a general rule, exceptions.

00:43:25.169 --> 00:43:26.704
There's a rule in
law, what is it?

00:43:26.704 --> 00:43:28.405
Bad cases make bad law.

00:43:30.207 --> 00:43:32.576
Nothing can cover
every instance.

00:43:32.576 --> 00:43:35.279
Do we all acknowledge that
we should stop at red lights?

00:43:35.279 --> 00:43:36.580
Yes.

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:39.450
How about if the guy
next to you in the car,

00:43:39.450 --> 00:43:41.452 align:start size:81% position:19%
your passenger
has a heart attack

00:43:41.452 --> 00:43:43.554
and it's a matter of time

00:43:43.554 --> 00:43:45.556
that you have to get
him to a hospital.

00:43:45.556 --> 00:43:48.559
Will you go through a red
light if you can? Yes.

00:43:48.559 --> 00:43:50.828
Will everybody justify it? Yes.

00:43:50.828 --> 00:43:54.264
So we all understand there
are exceptions to rules,

00:43:54.264 --> 00:43:56.734
but it takes a lot

00:43:56.734 --> 00:43:59.303
and you just have to
